          Latex-ite is a good choice for a sound, moderately weathered blacktop driveway that sees car traffic, a few oil drips and a normal northern winter. It is not a repair product, it does not rescue a driveway full of alligator cracking, and it will not outperform a pro-grade sealcoat sprayed by a contractor. Buy it for what it is: a thick, polymer-fortified DIY coating that rewards careful prep and punishes shortcuts.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="who-makes-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Who Makes Latex-ite and What the Brand Stands For
          </h2>

          <p>
            Latex-ite is the driveway brand of Dalton Enterprises, a Connecticut company that has been selling asphalt maintenance products to homeowners and contractors for decades. The name comes from the brand's long-standing pitch: blacktop sealer modified with polymer (originally latex rubber) so the dried film flexes with the pavement instead of cracking like a brittle paint layer. That is the core promise you are paying for, and it is a reasonable one, because the number one way a cheap sealer fails is by drying hard, crazing in the first winter and flaking off by the second spring.
          </p>

          <p>
            The brand is aimed at the do-it-yourself buyer who wants something better than the cheapest pail on the pallet but does not want to hire a sealcoating crew. You will find it at large home centers and many regional hardware chains, usually in 4.75-gallon pails, stacked next to Black Jack, Rust-Oleum EpoxyShield and store-brand options. Stock is seasonal: shelves fill in April and thin out by late September in most of the country.
          </p>

          <p>
            One thing worth checking before you buy is the base binder. Latex-ite labels and safety data sheets state what each formula is made of, and some jurisdictions restrict refined coal tar sealers outright. If you live in Minnesota, Washington, or a town with a local ban, read the SDS for the exact pail on the shelf rather than assuming. Asphalt-based emulsions are the safe default for anyone with kids, pets, or a lawn that drains off the driveway.
          </p>

          <p>
            The short version of the brand is this: Latex-ite sells mid-priced, filler-rich sealers that go on thick, look deep black when fresh, and are labeled for resistance to gasoline and motor oil. Those are the three things most homeowners care about, so it is easy to see why the flagship has stayed on shelves so long. The rest of this review is about whether those strengths match your driveway.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="airport-grade" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Airport Grade Up Close: What the Label Actually Promises
          </h2>

          <p>
            Latex-ite Airport Grade is the product most people mean when they say Latex-ite. It is sold as a filler sealer, which means the liquid carries fine mineral filler that settles into surface pores and hairline cracks as you squeegee. That filler is why it feels heavier and goes on thicker than the bargain pails, and it is also why it covers fewer square feet per pail. You are buying more solids, not more water.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Airport Grade name is a marketing nod to fuel-resistant coatings used on airfield aprons, where spilled jet fuel and hydraulic fluid would dissolve an ordinary asphalt surface. On a home driveway the practical meaning is narrower: the cured film is labeled to resist gasoline and oil drips better than an unmodified emulsion. That matters if you park an older car that weeps oil, a motorcycle, or a lawn tractor you fuel on the driveway.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">READ THIS FIRST</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Fuel resistant is not fuel proof.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A label claim of gas and oil resistance means a spill wiped up within a reasonable time will not soften the coating. A puddle left to soak for a week will still stain and can still lift the film. Keep a bag of clay absorbent in the garage and clean drips the same day.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Drying and cure times follow the same rules as every emulsion sealer. The label generally asks for air and pavement temperatures of at least 50 degrees Fahrenheit and rising, no rain for roughly 24 hours, and a wait of a day or two before driving on it. In shade, humidity, or cool fall weather, add time. Our guide on <a href="/how-long-for-driveway-sealer-to-dry/">how long driveway sealer takes to dry</a> covers the variables in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            What the label does not promise is crack repair. The filler bridges hairline checking, but anything wider than roughly an eighth of an inch needs a dedicated filler first. Pour sealer over open cracks and you get a black skin that splits again in the first freeze, with water still reaching the base underneath.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="the-lineup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Picking the Right Product Within the Latex-ite Line
          </h2>

          <p>
            The Latex-ite name covers more than one pail. Alongside the sealers, the brand sells crack fillers in pourable and rope form and a trowel-grade patch for shallow potholes and broken edges. Exact names and formulas change from season to season and between retailers, so treat the table below as a map of product types rather than a promise that every SKU is on every shelf.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What it does</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Use it when</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Filler sealer (Airport Grade)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Thick, fuel-resistant topcoat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Driveway is sound, mildly weathered, sees car traffic</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Premium or extended-life sealer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Heavier polymer load, longer service claim</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">You want fewer recoats and accept a higher pail price</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Rope crack filler (Pli-Stix)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Melted into cracks with a torch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cracks are roughly 1/8 to 1/2 inch wide</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pourable crack filler</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Squeezed or poured into cracks</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Many thin cracks, no torch on hand</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Trowel patch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fills shallow holes and edges</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Spot damage under about an inch deep</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            For most buyers the decision comes down to the standard filler sealer versus whatever premium formula the store carries that season. The premium versions usually carry more polymer and a longer service-life claim, and cost noticeably more per pail. If you seal every two years anyway, the cheaper pail is the better value. If you know you will stretch to three or four years, the premium pail is worth a look.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rope filler deserves a specific mention. Pli-Stix-style rope filler is pressed into the crack and melted with a propane torch, which gives a flexible, rubberized seal that holds better through freeze-thaw than most pourable fillers. It is more work and requires care around the torch, but on a driveway with a dozen medium cracks it is the most durable DIY fix in the aisle. Compare it against the alternatives in our <a href="/best-driveway-crack-filler/">crack filler rankings</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not mix brands blindly in the same coat. Using a Latex-ite filler under another brand's sealer is fine once the filler has cured, but pouring two different sealers into one bucket to stretch the job invites separation and uneven color. Buy enough of one product to finish the whole surface.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="versus-rivals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Latex-ite Compares With the Pails Next to It
          </h2>

          <p>
            In the big-box aisle Latex-ite usually competes with three kinds of product: thin budget emulsions that cost much less and cover more area, other filler sealers in the same price band, and premium polymer-modified pails that cost more and claim longer life. Where it lands depends on what you value more, coverage per dollar or film build per coat.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Option</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Film build</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Coverage per pail</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best for</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Budget emulsion</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Thin</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Highest</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Annual touch-ups on good asphalt</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Latex-ite Airport Grade</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Medium to thick</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Weathered drives, oil drips</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Rust-Oleum EpoxyShield Blacktop</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Medium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Similar use, often priced a little lower</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Black Jack Drive-Maxx</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Thick</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lower</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rough, porous surfaces, longest service claim</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Against a budget emulsion, Latex-ite wins on durability and appearance but loses on cost per square foot. On a smooth, young driveway where you only need a light refresh, the budget pail is honestly fine and saves money. On a driveway that has gone gray and porous, the thin product soaks in and disappears, and you end up buying two coats anyway.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against Black Jack's premium polymer pails, Latex-ite is usually cheaper per pail and easier to spread, while the heavier Black Jack products build more film and carry longer service claims. Against Rust-Oleum EpoxyShield Blacktop, the two are close enough that price on the day and what your store has in stock should decide it. For a full ranked comparison across brands, see our <a href="/best-asphalt-driveway-sealer-reviews/">asphalt driveway sealer rankings</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more comparison matters: Latex-ite versus hiring a sealcoating crew. Contractors spray commercial-grade emulsion, often with added sand and polymer, from bulk tanks. On a large driveway the per-square-foot price can be close to what you would spend on premium pails, without the labor. Our explainer on <a href="/asphalt-sealcoating/">asphalt sealcoating</a> breaks down when the pro route is the better deal.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="doing-it-right" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Getting a Latex-ite Job Right the First Time
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every bad Latex-ite review traces back to the same handful of prep mistakes, not the product. Start with a dry, clean surface: sweep, blow off grit, and pressure wash or scrub with a driveway cleaner, then give it a full day of sun to dry. Sealer applied over dust bonds to the dust, and the dust lets go of the asphalt.
          </p>

          <p>
            Treat oil spots before sealing. Scrub them with a degreaser, rinse, and if the stain is still tacky, prime it with an oil-spot primer. Emulsion sealer will not bond to oil-soaked asphalt, and you will see bald patches within weeks. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-remove-oil-stains-from-driveway/">removing oil stains from a driveway</a> lists the methods that actually work.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Mixing and spreading</h3>

          <p>
            The filler in Latex-ite settles hard at the bottom of the pail during storage. Stir it with a drill-mounted mixing paddle for several minutes until there is no sludge left on the bottom, and keep stirring every time you refill your working area. Unmixed sealer goes on thin and watery at the start of the pail and thick and gritty at the end, which shows as streaks and gray spots.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cut in the edges and along the garage apron with a brush, then pour a ribbon of sealer across the width of the driveway and pull it with a squeegee in overlapping passes. Work in sections of a few hundred square feet, keep a wet edge, and finish each section with strokes in the same direction for an even look. Two thin coats, with the first fully dry before the second, beat one thick coat every time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weather sets the schedule. Aim for a stretch of warm, dry days with pavement temperatures above 50 degrees and no rain in the forecast for at least a day, ideally two. Our article on the <a href="/best-time-to-seal-driveway/">best time to seal a driveway</a> covers the seasonal window by region.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pails-and-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Many Pails You Need and What It Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Filler sealers cover less area than thin emulsions because the solids stay on the surface. As a planning number, expect roughly 250 to 350 square feet per 4.75-gallon pail per coat on average asphalt, with the low end on rough, porous, older pavement and the high end on smooth, recently sealed surfaces. Always check the coverage range printed on the pail you buy, since it changes between formulas.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Planning Numbers for a Two-Car Driveway</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">600 sq ft</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical surface area</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2-3 pails</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per coat, depending on porosity</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">4-6 pails</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">For two full coats</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">50°F+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Minimum pavement temperature</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Pail prices move with the season and the retailer, but Latex-ite filler sealer has generally sold in the mid-range for a 4.75-gallon pail, with premium formulas costing more. For a 600-square-foot driveway with two coats, that puts the material bill somewhere in the low-to-mid hundreds of dollars before you add a squeegee, brush, mixing paddle and crack filler. Our breakdown of <a href="/how-much-does-it-cost-to-seal-a-driveway/">what it costs to seal a driveway</a> compares that with contractor pricing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy one more pail than your calculation says. A short pail mid-job forces a store run with half the driveway wet, and a partial second batch can dry to a slightly different shade. Most home centers accept unopened pails back within their return window, so the extra pail is cheap insurance rather than a sunk cost.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not stretch coverage by thinning with water unless the label explicitly allows a specific dilution. Extra water lowers the solids content you paid for, weakens the film and shortens its life. If the math says you are short, buy more product instead of watering down what you have.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="owner-reports" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What Owners Report After a Season or Two
          </h2>

          <p>
            Owner feedback on Latex-ite Airport Grade follows a consistent pattern. The most common praise is appearance: a rich, even black finish that makes an old driveway look new for the first season. Buyers also commonly mention that small hairline cracks disappear under two coats and that the surface sheds water noticeably better afterward.
          </p>

          <p>
            The most common complaints are about workload and longevity expectations. The product is heavy and thick, and owners with large driveways often say spreading it is more physical work than they expected. Some reviewers report wear in tire tracks and turning areas within the first year, which is typical of any DIY sealer on a surface where cars turn their wheels while stationary.
          </p>

          <p>
            Peeling and flaking reports almost always describe the same conditions: sealer applied over dust, over a recently washed surface that had not dried, in cool weather, or too thick in a single coat. Those are application issues, and they will cause the same failure with any emulsion. A few owners also mention tracking, where sealer that had not fully cured was carried into the garage on tires or shoes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Longevity claims from owners range widely, which is honest data in itself. Driveways in full sun with light traffic and good prep commonly go two to three years between coats. Shaded driveways, heavy trucks and poorly prepped surfaces see less. If you expect a single coat to last five years on a busy driveway, no pail in this price class will meet that expectation.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Who Should Buy Latex-ite and Who Should Skip It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy Latex-ite Airport Grade if your asphalt is structurally sound, has started to fade and get porous, and you want a thicker, more fuel-resistant coat than a budget pail delivers. It is a particularly sensible choice for driveways where a car or small engine regularly drips oil, and for owners willing to put in a careful prep day before the sealing day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if your driveway has widespread alligator cracking, soft spots, potholes or edges breaking away. Those are base failures, and no sealer of any brand will fix them; you are looking at patching, an overlay or replacement. Our guide to the <a href="/sealing-asphalt-driveway-pros-and-cons/">pros and cons of sealing asphalt</a> helps decide whether sealing is worth doing at all on a tired surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            Also skip it on brand-new asphalt. Fresh pavement needs months to cure out its oils before any sealer should go on, and sealing too early traps those oils and can leave the surface soft. Our article on <a href="/when-should-you-seal-a-new-asphalt-driveway/">when to seal a new asphalt driveway</a> gives the waiting period.
          </p>

          <p>
            And if you simply want the lowest cost per square foot to keep a young, smooth driveway looking dark, a thin budget emulsion every year or two will cost less and do the job. Latex-ite earns its price on surfaces that need the extra solids. On surfaces that do not, you are paying for filler you do not need. If you are still weighing brands, our <a href="/best-driveway-sealer/">best driveway sealer picks</a> and the store-specific guides for <a href="/driveway-sealer-home-depot/">Home Depot</a> and <a href="/driveway-sealer-menards/">Menards</a> show what else is on the shelf.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the filler on the bottom of the pail</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Latex-ite carries mineral filler that settles into a dense layer during storage. If you do not stir it with a drill paddle until the bottom is clear, the first half of the pail goes on watery and the last half goes on like mud, leaving streaks, thin spots and a coat that wears unevenly within months.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using sealer as a crack filler</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The filler bridges hairline checking, nothing more. Cracks wider than about an eighth of an inch need a rope or pourable crack filler first, allowed to cure fully. Sealer poured into an open crack shrinks, splits at the first freeze, and lets water keep working into the base below.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing the morning after a wash</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pressure-washed asphalt holds water in its pores for many hours, longer in shade. Sealer laid on a damp surface cannot bond and often peels in sheets by the next summer. Give the driveway at least a full sunny day to dry, and check shaded edges by hand before you start.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting it on in one thick coat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A single heavy coat to save time tends to skin over on top while staying soft underneath, then cracks and tracks. Two thinner coats, with the first dry to the touch and firm before the second, cure harder and last longer for the same amount of product.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the forecast too far ahead</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Emulsion needs roughly a day without rain and warm temperatures to set. Checking the forecast once, three days early, is how most washed-off jobs happen. Check the hourly forecast the morning you start, and postpone if there is a meaningful chance of rain or a cold night.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Latex-ite Airport Grade a coal tar sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Check the safety data sheet for the exact pail you are buying, because formulas and regional versions can differ and the label is the only reliable source. Several states and many towns restrict refined coal tar sealers, so retailers in those areas should only stock compliant products. If the SDS lists asphalt emulsion as the binder, it is the lower-toxicity type most homeowners should prefer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does Latex-ite last on a driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Owner reports most commonly put a well-applied two-coat job at about two to three years before it needs a refresh, with longer service in full sun and light traffic. Tire tracks, turning areas and shaded sections wear first. Poor prep, cool weather or a single thick coat shortens that sharply. Plan on resealing on a two to three year cycle rather than hoping for five.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I put Latex-ite over an old sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if the old coat is sound and well bonded. Scrape and sweep away any flaking material, clean the surface thoroughly, and let it dry. Applying over an old coat that is peeling simply adds weight to a failing layer. If the old sealer was a different chemistry, such as a solvent-based product, test a small area first to make sure the new coat bonds.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many pails of Latex-ite do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Measure length times width of the paved area and divide by the coverage on the pail label, typically somewhere around 250 to 350 square feet per pail per coat for filler sealers. Use the low end of the range for rough or porous asphalt. Double the number for two coats, then add one spare pail you can return unopened if you do not need it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What happens if it rains after I apply Latex-ite?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Rain within the first few hours can wash uncured sealer off the surface, leaving gray streaks and a mess in the gutter. Rain after the surface has skinned over usually causes only minor spotting. Let it dry completely, sweep off any loose residue, and touch up or recoat affected areas. Our article on rain after driveway sealing explains how to judge the damage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Latex-ite work on concrete driveways?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Asphalt emulsion sealers are made to bond with and protect asphalt, and on concrete they leave a black coating that peels and looks out of place. Concrete needs a penetrating silane or siloxane repellent or an acrylic concrete sealer instead. Use the product designed for the surface you actually have.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long before I can drive on a Latex-ite coat?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Foot traffic is usually fine after several hours in warm, sunny weather, but vehicle traffic generally needs at least 24 to 48 hours, and longer in cool, humid or shaded conditions. Turning your wheels while parked on a fresh coat is the fastest way to scuff it. Block the driveway entrance with tape or a sawhorse so nobody pulls in early.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

